# Load helper functions
load('ext://seal', 'seal_service', 'publish_images')

# Same registry the root Tiltfile passes to default_registry() (see its own
# comment there) -- config.Tiltfile's own docstring is explicit that
# registry selection is this project's job, not the seal package's, so this
# has to be repeated here rather than looked up from Tilt itself (Tilt
# doesn't expose default_registry()'s value back to Starlark). Only used by
# seal_service()'s SEAL_IMAGE_TAG-gated stable-tag push (see
# /tilt/docker_build/README.md) -- irrelevant anywhere but a pipeline
# actually publishing a build, since SEAL_IMAGE_TAG is otherwise unset and
# this is never read.
_REGISTRY = 'ghcr.io/your-org' if publish_images else None

live_update=[
    # Sync Python files for live reload
    sync('./src', '/app/src'),
    # Restart the Django server when model files change
    run('uv run python src/manage.py migrate', trigger=['/app/src/**/models.py']),
]

# 'example-api' is this service's one identifier, everywhere: the
# 'image:' placeholder and container name hardcoded in
# k8s/dev/web/api/deployment.yaml, and its 'example-api-secrets'
# Secret. The root Tiltfile's default_registry() call handles pushing the
# image to the right registry; naming it here is this project's own
# responsibility.
#
# seal_service() turns that service's .env into a k8s Secret (see
# /rfcs/0006-credential-resolution.md), builds whichever of this service's own
# Dockerfile stages the run's build type asks for, and -- because test_stage
# is given -- registers this service's test pipeline. The three stage names
# below are this Dockerfile's own; seal assumes none of them.
#
# `test_command` is what produces this service's report, run inside the
# container once it is up rather than while the image is built. That is what
# gives these tests the Postgres this service is actually configured
# against.
#
# The `reset` below runs on both sides of that -- once so these tests start
# from the baseline, once so the outcome suite does whatever they did. This
# suite reads neither: pytest-django creates a `test_`-prefixed database of
# its own and drops it afterwards. This container is merged into the shared 'web'
# Deployment/pod (see k8s/dev/web), so point k8s_resource_name and
# resource_dep at 'web', while service_name (also this container's own name
# in that pod) keeps identifying this service's own tests-results/ directory
# and its own seal_tests_verdict_example_api resource.
#
# `reset` is this service's own script for putting the state it owns --
# Postgres tables and its Valkey broker -- back to a known baseline.
# Declaring it here is what registers the 'seal_reset_example_api'
# resource to trigger, and, in a test run, the
# 'seal_tests_reset_example_api' that restores the same baseline ahead of
# this service's own tests. `ui` declares none: it owns no state to put
# back.
seal_service(
    'example-api',
    context='.',
    dockerfile='Dockerfile',
    live_update=live_update,
    k8s_resource_name='deploy/web',
    resource_dep='web',
    development_stage='development',
    runtime_stage='runtime',
    test_stage='test',
    junit_tests_directory='/app/tests-results/',
    test_command='make unit-tests',
    registry=_REGISTRY,
    reset='deploy/reset.sh',
)
